{:tier :glm-5.2, :verse 9, :comments ["<b>The enemy said</b> — to his people when he enticed them with words: \"I will pursue them, I will overtake them, and I will divide the spoil with my officers and my servants.\" This is the language of Rashi (Rashi on Exodus 15:9). And I saw in Midrash Chazita thus: Rabbi Yishmael taught — <b>The enemy said, I will pursue, I will overtake</b> — this should have been the beginning of the song; and why was it not written [first]? Because there is no earlier and later in the Torah (see Kohelet Rabbah 1:12). And Onkelos (Onkelos on Exodus 15:9) is of the same opinion, and therefore he translated \"דַּהֲוָה אָמַר שָׂנְאָה\" (was the enemy saying), referring to the beginning of his intent when he pursued after them.\n\nAnd in my opinion, according to the plain sense, it is arranged in its proper sequence in the verse as it stands. For at first (Exodus 15:4) he said that they were sunk in the sea and went down into the depths, and this was when the waters turned back and covered the chariots and the horsemen (Exodus 15:5); and afterwards (Exodus 15:8–10) he returned to tell how this came about: for by the breath of Your nostrils — a mighty east wind — the waters were heaped up at first and the depths froze; and because of this the enemy thought he would pursue and overtake them in the sea and divide their spoil and fill himself with them; but You blew with Your breath and the sea covered them. And he mentioned this because even in this plan of his there was a cause and a wonder from HaShem, who strengthened their hearts and confounded their counsel to enter the sea, as I explained above (14:4, 21). Therefore after this he said: Who is like You among the mighty (בָּאֵלִם) — performing great and wondrous deeds through a word and its reversal."], :book :exodus, :voice :ramban, :lang :en, :chapter 15, :notes {}, :source-witnesses [{"versionTitle" "Sefaria community Hebrew (PD)", "language" "he"}], :timestamp "2026-07-22T03:08:27.769989492Z", :translated-from :he, :model "glm-5.2"}